Z||000002||sombre||08-07-2001||11:43 PM||||try seting up somewhere with some indirest lighting....Like a shady corner of a big parking lot where the light just don't quite reach...This will lower your exposure times but still let the car show up and the neon...<P>  I used to take pics of my camaro at the very end of our driveway with our motion light on on the house.This gave enough light for good clear pics but still datk enough for good results...took a few rolls of film to figure out but it looks good in the end..<P>   BTW: Is the car silver????If so I'm gonna have to retink my paint plans....I thought silver with blue would look good guess now I know....<BR>                                   Greg||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||08-07-2001||11:55 PM||||better watch out for speedbumps..... it'd make a mess out of neon tubes..<P>btw, looks good!<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||08-08-2001||01:02 AM||||btw, my sister's really good with photography, here's a trick I learned...<P>put the camera on a tripod and take about a 5 second time exposure, with your car in the dark (neons on..) then, without advancing the film, take another picture, regular speed, this time with the flash on.<P>makes an interesting effect..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
